ql database in 2017. to show that  datastore joined forces with firebase  the product was rebranded as cloud  firestore  oh yeah and you told me to wait until  this video to learn more so here we are  you also mentioned that firestore is  fully compatible with datastore and that  all datastore databases will be  converted to firestore under the covers  is that right  ah yeah martin once converted they will  be operating in datastore compatibility  mode or firestore in datastore mode for  short this mode however does not give  you access to firestore's native  features only data stores in order to  access all of firestore you need to be  running in firestore native mode  similarly when prompted to create a new  nosql datastore you get to choose  between firestore and datastore mode or  firestore in native mode  check out the chart in the docs to see  the differences between them oh i see so  all users need to do is switch from  firestore in data store mode to  native mode and i good right  but i think i'd better ask now what's  the bad news you mentioned yeah i think  you know what i'm gonna say  you can't switch between datastore and  native firestore modes once the mode is  set and data is stored the mode is  locked also the compound matters  datastore and firestore are mutually  exclusive in gcp projects meaning that  once the mode is locked not only can you  not change it but in order to use the  other mode you need to actually create a  brand new project  so what you're saying is if i got data  in datastore  even if i migrate to the cloud ndb or  cloud datastore client libraries i'm not  able to use cloud firestore in native  mode unless i  create a new project with a new app  select firestore in native mode in that  new project  dump the data store data out of my  current project  and load it into firestore in the new  project  yep you got it and now you know why this  is even more optional than moving from  cloud ndb to cloud datastore at least  with that migration you don't have to  create a brand new project but this one  does require that and it doesn't matter  if it's an app engine app cloud run or  even a non-cloud app using datastore you  have to have a new project  so once again you really need to have a  good reason to make this migration and  if accessing those firebase features is  compelling enough you got to do what you  got to do right  yep that's right well i do want to take  advantage of firestore's native features  and i'm willing to create a new project  to make it happen  how do i get started well the first  thing is you must recognize there are  two pieces to this puzzle  first your data must be transferred from  datastore in your current project to  firestore in the new project that's part  one  secondly it probably goes without saying  that you need an app in that new project  that uses the firestore client library  okay i got it but before we do that i  remember from what is was it module  three that the cloud datastore client  library can be used by both app engine  and non-app engine apps is that true for  cloud firestore as well  yeah definitely martin while we're going  to demo migrating an app engine app to  cloud firestore it could be any app  really the only real requirement is that  you have to have a new project and since  you mentioned module 3 that's where we  created our cloud datastore app so we're  going to migrate that one to cloud  firestore specifically the python 3  version as python 2 apps are more likely  to be using cloud ndb instead so if you  did the codelab use your code or grab  ours from the module 3b folder by either  cloning the repo or downloading the zip  file pause here if you need to but once  you're ready let's go to the computer  now  okay before we start we need another  cloud project for firestore so make a  new one in the cloud console or reuse an  existing one where neither data store  nor firestore are used grab that  project's id not the project name nor  number the id then enable the cloud  firestore api if you prefer the command  line use gcloud projects create then  change to that new project with gcloud  config set and finally turn on firestore  with gcloud services enable of course  you'll be asked to enable billing as  well since we always suggest you start  with working code deploy the module 3  python 3 app whether you're using yours  or ours but do this from your old  project the one using datastore  use gcloud config list to see your  current project and use gcloud config  set if necessary once you're on your  data store project run gcloud app deploy  and confirm the app works like before  now switch to your new project with  gcloud config set project one more time  as we'll be here the rest of this  migration starting with the  configuration files  the only config you need to change is to  switch from cloud data store or cloud  ndb if you're using that to cloud  firestore there should be a one-line  swap in requirements.txt  all other changes are in main.py replace  the cloud datastore client library  imports and api client instantiation at  the top with the same for cloud  firestore groups of like entities in  datastore are identified with a common  parent key entities in datastore are  referred to as documents in firestore  entities are of a certain kind in  datastore while firestore documents are  grouped as collections when starting a  new visit in datastore a new entity is  created with the visit parent key  likewise on the firestore site we set  our collection to the same name as a  datastore parent key the datastore  entity gets its data key value pairs in  the same way that a firestore document  does but the latter gets added to the  collection immediately while the  datastore entity has to be written  explicitly next are the datastore  queries where you query for visit  entitykinds the desired ordering then  fetch and return for firestore query the  visit collection specify the ordering  fetch and convert to python friendly  dictionaries then return as a memory  friendly iterable believe it or not that  wraps the changes necessary to switch  from cloud data store to cloud firestore  the real work is in having to create a  new project so what do you think of that  migration martin  that migration wasn't too bad at all  but earlier you said any app can use  cloud firestore if i'm willing to create  a new project to access firestore and  take the effort to migrate my data over  does this mean i can rewrite my python 2  data store app as a node.js firestore  app absolutely martin your data store  app could be app engine cloud run or  even non-cloud it's not like the old app  engine days where if you're using  datastore it had to be an app engine app  that's certainly not the case anymore so  the same applies to your new project  meaning you can have any app using  firestore as its database  so if your company is doing more work in  node.js today there's nothing wrong with  rebuilding or refactoring as a node 16  cloud runout for your new firestore  project that's what we mean by app  engine and really all of google cloud as  a whole being more open than ever before  for both your data and for your apps  that's really interesting i've been  thinking about doing exactly that as my  team is doing more with node.js these  days anyway now that i know how to  migrate my app to firestore what about  migrating my data  definitely martin we're going to tackle  that in module 10. while you're waiting  for the data migration sql here are some  additional resources to consider  earlier we link you to a chart  differentiating datastore in firestore  native modes above that chart is our  guidance on which of those modes to  choose for your apps so check that page  in the docs and you know just look over  all of the firestore docs anyway to  become familiar with your brand new  nosql database  also check out this other video from one  of our colleagues that dives deeper into  the migration and the differences  between datastore and firestore cool  i'll check those out  now are there other migrations i should  consider for my app engine app well  first you can see from this list we've  already migrated from our old app into  nato store and ndb to cloud nab cloud  data store and now cloud firestore  however if you're on cloud neb you can  migrate directly to cloud firestore  there's no need to migrate first to  cloud datastore and then go from  datastore to firestore just combine  module 3 with this module 6.  as far as other migrations go if you're  thinking about containerizing your app  engine apps to run on cloud run go and  check out modules four and five  modules seven eight and nine help you  learn about how to migrate from app  engine task queue to cloud tasks  finally if you've got a small app that's  just one function or a big app that you  want to break up into multiple  microservices consider migrating to  cloud functions you'll find that in  module 11. ah thanks for the pointers  there are so many ways to upgrade  oh that's definitely true we want to  give our users lots of options and thank  you viewers for staying with us on these  journeys modernizing your serverless  apps this is wesley from google cloud on  behalf of martin importer hoping you'll  join us soon at the next migration  station or on a different serverless  expedition soon please like and  subscribe to see more content like this  and until next time happy travels  [Music]  you

